      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we need to consider the given probabilities and the fact that the events are independent.\n\nThe probability it will rain on Saturday is \\( P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) = 60\\% = 0.60 \\).\nThe probability it will rain on Sunday is \\( P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 25\\% = 0.25 \\).\n\nSince the events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both days is the product of the individual probabilities for each day. Therefore, the probability it will rain on both days is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{Rain on Saturday and Sunday}) = P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\times P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 0.60 \\times 0.25\n\\]\n\nNow, let's perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n0.60 \\times 0.25 = \\frac{60}{100} \\times \\frac{25}{100} = \\frac{60 \\times 25}{100 \\times 100} = \\frac{1500}{10000} = \\frac{15}{100} = 0.15\n\\]\n\nFinally, we convert the decimal 0.15 back to a percentage by multiplying by 100:\n\n\\[\n0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\%\n\\]\n\nSo, the probability it will rain on both days is \\( \\boxed{15\\%} \\).",
