    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday, we use the rule of independent events in probability. The probability of two independent events both occurring is the product of their individual probabilities.\n\nGiven:\n- The probability it will rain on Saturday, \\(P(R_S) = 60\\% = 0.6\\).\n- The probability it will rain on Sunday, \\(P(R_U) = 25\\% = 0.25\\).\n\nSince the events are independent, the probability that it will rain on both days is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\nP(R_S \\cap R_U) = P(R_S) \\times P(R_U) = 0.6 \\times 0.25\n\\]\n\nNow let's calculate the product.\n\n\\[\n0.6 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\n\\]\n\nConvert this probability to a percentage:\n\n\\[\n0.15 \\times 100\\% = 15\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that it will rain on both days is \\(\\boxed{15\\%}\\).",
