      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "- It is first necessary to determine the likelihood of an event happening and not happening, which can be found using the complement rule. The probability of not raining on Saturday is $100\\% - 60\\% = 40\\%$.\n- The probability of not raining on Sunday is $100\\% - 25\\% = 75\\%$.\n- The probability it will only rain on one day is given by the product of the probability it will rain on that day and the probability it will not rain on the other day. Thus, it will only rain on Saturday with a probability of $60\\% \\cdot 40\\% = 0.24$, and it will only rain on Sunday with a probability of $25\\% \\cdot 75\\% = 0.1875$.\n- The probability it will rain on both days must be subtracted from the sum of the two individual probabilities. Therefore, it is $0.24 + 0.1875 - (0.24 + 0.1875 - 0.24 \\cdot 0.1875) = 0.4245916667$, where the parentheses are to remind you to add back up to the starting point before subtracting. \n\nOnly $42\\%$ of the days will have a rain tomorrow. $\\boxed{42\\%}$",
